WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.680
A buffer is a contiguous region intended to hold a specific amount of data.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:10.500
It may hold bytes, characters, records, integers, structures, or other elements.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.680
Whatever it holds, the buffer has finite capacity.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.780
Safe code must know that capacity before writing or reading through a boundary-sensitive operation.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.440
C arrays do not automatically carry runtime capacity information into every expression or function call.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.660
Inside the scope where an array is declared, the compiler may know its size.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:36.160
Once the array is passed to a function as a pointer, that size information is no longer automatically available through the pointer alone.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:39.900
Array-to-pointer decay can hide the original size of a buffer.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:46.680
A function receiving a pointer often cannot tell whether it points to one element, ten elements, or a larger region.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:52.100
That is why safe C interfaces commonly pass capacity or length alongside the pointer.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.080
Pointer arithmetic must stay within the valid object or array boundary.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.580
Element counts and byte counts are not interchangeable.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:05.960
A buffer that holds ten integers does not have the same byte capacity as a buffer that holds ten characters.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.220
Reviewers need to know which unit a length represents.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:18.800
A safe buffer operation needs three pieces of information: the destination capacity, the intended number of elements or bytes to process, and the resulting length.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.220
If any of those are missing or guessed, the operation is harder to review and easier to misuse.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.800
External input should never be assumed to fit into a fixed buffer.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:35.940
Files, sockets, devices, arguments, environment values, and records from other systems may all exceed local assumptions.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:41.280
Defensive code checks the available space before copying, formatting, parsing, or appending.
